+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I sell my house without a real estate agent in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The state's license law has an exception for an owner selling their own home. A South Carolina attorney still supervises the closing, and the buyer gets the state's disclosure form before the contract."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who pays the deed stamps when you sell by owner in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The seller pays them, whether or not an agent is involved. The fee is $1.85 for each $500 of the price: $1,295 on a $350,000 sale."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I sell my house to my son or daughter without the disclosure form?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, if the sale is only to your children, grandchildren, parents or grandparents. If your child's spouse is also a buyer, the sale needs the form."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I have to tell a buyer that someone died in the house?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. South Carolina does not require it. If a buyer asks you directly, you may not lie about it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I sell my house as is in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. An as-is sale still needs the state's disclosure form, unless the sale is exempt for another reason."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a buyer use their own agent to buy a for-sale-by-owner home?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The buyer's written agreement with their agent says how that agent is paid. You can offer to pay the buyer's agent, or offer the buyer a credit, but no rule requires it."}}]}</script>
